{
	title: 'Refresh browser state immediately after wake',
	spec: 'Browser activity indicators describe confirmed live state. Returning from sleep, a hidden page, restored page or lost network immediately starts a fresh connection without waiting for the old socket to close. A local execution-gap check catches sleep without lifecycle events, within about one second after script execution resumes; it sends no periodic network requests. Coalesce overlapping wake signals. Reopen the shown session before other followed sessions and preserve pending command IDs and draft state. Retain cards and reading position until fresh history covers the previously loaded range; fetch missing older pages with the existing history protocol, fold live events into the replacement offscreen and reveal it together. Repeat reconnects restart that refresh from the last confirmed loaded range. Never resurrect cached history that the host rewrote. Suspend Hal cursor, spinner, status pulse and tab-marker animations while stale, and show Updating while connected or Reconnecting while disconnected. A socket opening alone and background snapshots cannot certify the shown session as fresh. A disconnect repaints immediately. Browser-only behavior; terminal wake behavior stays unchanged. No new dependencies. Tests cover replacement-connection isolation, command replay, wake decisions, freshness before versus after the visible snapshot and preserving loaded older history through reconnect.',
